keville 13S Mesonet        3.44 inches|Fishtrap Lake Gauge         3.01 inches|Virgie Gauge                    3.20 inches|Elkhorn City River Gauge    3.54 inches|Myra IFLOWS                   3.38 inches    |Virgie IFLOWS                  3.34 inches   |Beefhide IFLOWS              3.59 inches.', 'Numerous roads in Cranks, Evarts, and Harlan and elsewhere around the county were closed or impassable due to flooding. A written state of emergency was declared for Harlan County during the height of the flooding.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750367, 201806, 13, 1845, 201806, 13, 1845, 125103, 'FLORIDA', 12, 2018, 'June', 'Lightning', 'TBW', '13-JUN-18 18:45:00', 'EST-5', '13-JUN-18 18:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '500.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 62, 'N', 'MURDOCK', 62, 'N', 'MURDOCK', 27.8957, -82.3417, 27.8957, -82.3417, 'Scattered thunderstorms developed across the Florida Peninsula during the afternoon and evening hours. A lightning strike from one of the storms caused a fire at an apartment building in Hillsborough County.', 'Broadcast media widely reported on a fire at a 3 story apartment complex that was believed to be started by a lightning strike. The fire caused major damage to the roof and top floor of the building, damaging 24 units and displacing around 100 residents. No injuries were reported from the fire.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750374, 201806, 1, 756, 201806, 1, 806, 125104, 'GULF OF MEXICO', 85, 2018, 'June', 'Waterspout', 'TBW', '01-JUN-18 07:56:00', 'EST-5', '01-JUN-18 08:06:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Trained Spotter', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, 'SSW', 'ST PETERSBURG BEACH', 12, 'SSW', 'ST PETERSBURG BEACH', 27.56, -82.8, 27.5602, -82.8012, 'A small line of showers formed near the mouth of the Tampa Bay shortly after sunrise at the intersection of a land breeze and an old outflow boundary. A couple of waterspouts were reported with the showers.', 'Multiple reports and pictures were received of a waterspout, or perhaps two waterspouts, offshore of Egmont Key and Bean Point of Anna Maria Island. The location of the waterspout was estimated from radar.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (741863, 201802, 21, 1500, 201802, 22, 1600, 123641, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'February', 'Flood', 'ALY', '21-FEB-18 15:00:00', 'EST-5', '22-FEB-18 16:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', NULL, NULL, 'Ice Jam', NULL, 1, 'NE', 'ALPLAUS', 1, 'ENE', 'ALPLAUS', 42.857, -73.906, 42.854, -73.9057, 'A large ice jam remained in place on the Mohawk River for much of the month of February and was one of the biggest ice jam seen in decades, according to Union College professor John Garver. The ice jam stretched up to 17 miles from Rexford to Crane Hollow throughout the month in February. The ice jam mainly remained in place into late February and received a large amount of media and government attention. Albany County was concerned about impacts downstream if the jam were to give way quickly. Temperatures skyrocketing into the 60s and 70s on February 20-21 coupled with around 0.50-1.00 rainfall on the 19th over the Mohawk basin allowed river levels to rise and flooding occurred by the 21st within the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady and across the river in Scotia, as well as backwater flooding of the Alplaus Kill. A substantial portion of the ice jam gave way during the early morning hours of the 22nd, leading to concern for flooding in the downstream communities, but it turned out that impacts were minimal. There were some road closures in the Rotterdam Junction and Schenectady area due to the ice jam movement and breakage. The remainder of the jam gradually broke up over the next week or so with little impact.', 'There was backwater flooding along Alplaus Kill from the Mohawk River Ice Jam. Location was estimated.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (741569, 201802, 21, 400, 201802, 22, 1600, 123641, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'February', 'Flood', 'ALY', '21-FEB-18 04:00:00', 'EST-5', '22-FEB-18 16:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Ice Jam', NULL, 1, 'ESE', 'SCOTIA', 2, 'ESE', 'SCOTIA', 42.8207, -73.9447, 42.82, -73.9434, 'A large ice jam remained in place on the Mohawk River for much of the month of February and was one of the biggest ice jam seen in decades, according to Union College professor John Garver. The ice jam stretched up to 17 miles from Rexford to Crane Hollow throughout the month in February. The ice jam mainly remained in place into late February and received a large amount of media and government attention. Albany County was concerned about impacts downstream if the jam were to give way quickly. Temperatures skyrocketing into the 60s and 70s on February 20-21 coupled with around 0.50-1.00 rainfall on the 19th over the Mohawk basin allowed river levels to rise and flooding occurred by the 21st within the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady and across the river in Scotia, as well as backwater flooding of the Alplaus Kill. A substantial portion of the ice jam gave way during the early morning hours of the 22nd, leading to concern for flooding in the downstream communities, but it turned out that impacts were minimal. There were some road closures in the Rotterdam Junction and Schenectady area due to the ice jam movement and breakage. The remainder of the jam gradually broke up over the next week or so with little impact.', 'Flooding occurred in the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady from an ice jam.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750478, 201804, 15, 1818, 201804, 15, 1818, 125125, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'April', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'LWX', '15-APR-18 18:18:00', 'EST-5', '15-APR-18 18:18:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, '911 Call Center', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'W', 'ALLENS CREEK', 1, 'W', 'ALLENS CREEK', 37.5725, -78.8971, 37.5725, -78.8971, 'Low pressure and its associated cold front passed through on the evening and overnight hours of the 15th. A few thunderstorms were able to mix down strong winds from aloft, producing damaging wind gusts.', 'Three trees were down near the Intersection of Tye River Road and US-60.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (741864, 201802, 22, 1100, 201802, 23, 1100, 123641, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'February', 'Flood', 'ALY', '22-FEB-18 11:00:00', 'EST-5', '23-FEB-18 11:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Ice Jam', NULL, 3, 'SE', 'ATHOL', 3, 'ENE', 'ATHOL', 43.4755, -73.83, 43.5251, -73.8142, 'A large ice jam remained in place on the Mohawk River for much of the month of February and was one of the biggest ice jam seen in decades, according to Union College professor John Garver. The ice jam stretched up to 17 miles from Rexford to Crane Hollow throughout the month in February. The ice jam mainly remained in place into late February and received a large amount of media and government attention. Albany County was concerned about impacts downstream if the jam were to give way quickly. Temperatures skyrocketing into the 60s and 70s on February 20-21 coupled with around 0.50-1.00 rainfall on the 19th over the Mohawk basin allowed river levels to rise and flooding occurred by the 21st within the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady and across the river in Scotia, as well as backwater flooding of the Alplaus Kill. A substantial portion of the ice jam gave way during the early morning hours of the 22nd, leading to concern for flooding in the downstream communities, but it turned out that impacts were minimal. There were some road closures in the Rotterdam Junction and Schenectady area due to the ice jam movement and breakage. The remainder of the jam gradually broke up over the next week or so with little impact.', 'Emergency management reported flooding of River Road in the vicinity of Snack Rock on the Hudson River.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (771114, 201808, 28, 1602, 201808, 28, 1602, 128529, 'GULF OF MEXICO', 85, 2018, 'August', 'Marine Thunderstorm Wind', 'TBW', '28-AUG-18 16:02:00', 'EST-5', '28-AUG-18 16:02:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'ASOS', 37, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 8, 'NNE', 'OLD TAMPA BAY', 8, 'NNE', 'OLD TAMPA BAY', 27.9633, -82.54, 27.9633, -82.54, 'Numerous thunderstorms developed across Florida during the afternoon and shifted west into the eastern Gulf of Mexico during the late afternoon and early evening. Some of these storms produced gusty winds along the coast.', 'The ASOS at Tampa International Airport (KTPA) recorded a 37 knot thunderstorm wind gust.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (741565, 201802, 21, 400, 201802, 22, 2200, 123641, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'February', 'Flood', 'ALY', '21-FEB-18 04:00:00', 'EST-5', '22-FEB-18 22:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Ice Jam', NULL, 1, 'SE', 'SCOTIA', 1, 'SE', 'SCOTIA', 42.8223, -73.9566, 42.8217, -73.9558, 'A large ice jam remained in place on the Mohawk River for much of the month of February and was one of the biggest ice jam seen in decades, according to Union College professor John Garver. The ice jam stretched up to 17 miles from Rexford to Crane Hollow throughout the month in February. The ice jam mainly remained in place into late February and received a large amount of media and government attention. Albany County was concerned about impacts downstream if the jam were to give way quickly. Temperatures skyrocketing into the 60s and 70s on February 20-21 coupled with around 0.50-1.00 rainfall on the 19th over the Mohawk basin allowed river levels to rise and flooding occurred by the 21st within the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady and across the river in Scotia, as well as backwater flooding of the Alplaus Kill. A substantial portion of the ice jam gave way during the early morning hours of the 22nd, leading to concern for flooding in the downstream communities, but it turned out that impacts were minimal. There were some road closures in the Rotterdam Junction and Schenectady area due to the ice jam movement and breakage. The remainder of the jam gradually broke up over the next week or so with little impact.', 'Water backed up into the Jumpin Jacks parking lot due to an ice jam located at Freemans Bridge.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (753033, 201806, 17, 1939, 201806, 17, 1939, 125501, 'FLORIDA', 12, 2018, 'June', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'TBW', '17-JUN-18 19:39:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUN-18 19:39:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', 39, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'SSW', 'HOMOSASSA SPRINGS', 2, 'SSW', 'HOMOSASSA SPRINGS', 28.7697, -82.5545, 28.7697, -82.5545, 'Thunderstorms developed along sea breeze boundaries and pushed west into the Nature Coast during the early evening. One of these storms produced damaging wind gusts in Citrus County.', 'Citrus County Emergency Management reported some tree limbs and small branches were knocked down. A sign mounted on a metal pipe was also knocked down, but the pipe was reported to be old and rusted.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (753892, 201806, 25, 1718, 201806, 25, 1718, 125708, 'GULF OF MEXICO', 85, 2018, 'June', 'Marine Thunderstorm Wind', 'TBW', '25-JUN-18 17:18:00', 'EST-5', '25-JUN-18 17:18:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Buoy', 34, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 4, 'NNE', 'LOWER TAMPA BAY', 4, 'NNE', 'LOWER TAMPA BAY', 27.663, -82.618, 27.663, -82.618, 'Thunderstorms developed over the Florida Peninsula throughout the afternoon hours and pushed west towards the Gulf Coast through the early evening. Some of these storms produced marine wind gusts along the coast.', 'The buoy at Middle Tampa Bay (C-CUT) recorded a 34 knot marine thunderstorm wind gust.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (753895, 201806, 25, 1730, 201806, 25, 1730, 125708, 'GULF OF MEXICO', 85, 2018, 'June', 'Marine Thunderstorm Wind', 'TBW', '25-JUN-18 17:30:00', 'EST-5', '25-JUN-18 17:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Mesonet', 34, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 6, 'N', 'HILLSBOROUGH BAY', 6, 'N', 'HILLSBOROUGH BAY', 27.943, -82.4459, 27.943, -82.4459, 'Thunderstorms developed over the Florida Peninsula throughout the afternoon hours and pushed west towards the Gulf Coast through the early evening. Some of these storms produced marine wind gusts along the coast.', 'The PORTS site at Cruise Terminal 2 recorded a 34 knot marine thunderstorm wind gust.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (751660, 201805, 4, 2016, 201805, 4, 2016, 125334, 'VERMONT', 50, 2018, 'May', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'ALY', '04-MAY-18 20:16:00', 'EST-5', '04-MAY-18 20:16:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'Public', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'NNE', 'WEST BRATTLEBORO', 2, 'NNE', 'WEST BRATTLEBORO', 42.8914, -72.591, 42.8914, -72.591, 'A powerful low pressure system formed on May 4th, pushing a strong cold front across New York and New England. A line of thunderstorms developed along this front and pushed into southern Vermont in the evening, resulting in dozens of downed trees and wires. Over 2,500 customers lost power as a result of the storms.', 'A maple tree was uprooted and several large pine branches were downed. A barn roof was slightly damaged.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (741572, 201802, 22, 500, 201802, 22, 1000, 123641, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'February', 'Flood', 'ALY', '22-FEB-18 05:00:00', 'EST-5', '22-FEB-18 10:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Ice Jam', NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'SCOTIA GAY VLY ARPT', 1, 'S', 'SCOTIA GAY VLY ARPT', 42.8532, -74.0253, 42.8585, -74.0276, 'A large ice jam remained in place on the Mohawk River for much of the month of February and was one of the biggest ice jam seen in decades, according to Union College professor John Garver. The ice jam stretched up to 17 miles from Rexford to Crane Hollow throughout the month in February. The ice jam mainly remained in place into late February and received a large amount of media and government attention. Albany County was concerned about impacts downstream if the jam were to give way quickly. Temperatures skyrocketing into the 60s and 70s on February 20-21 coupled with around 0.50-1.00 rainfall on the 19th over the Mohawk basin allowed river levels to rise and flooding occurred by the 21st within the Stockade neighborhood of Schenectady and across the river in Scotia, as well as backwater flooding of the Alplaus Kill. A substantial portion of the ice jam gave way during the early morning hours of the 22nd, leading to concern for flooding in the downstream communities, but it turned out that impacts were minimal. There were some road closures in the Rotterdam Junction and Schenectady area due to the ice jam movement and breakage. The remainder of the jam gradually broke up over the next week or so with little impact.', 'All lanes of Route 5S E/W from NY-890 ramps to Mabie Lane were closed due to flooding from an ice jam.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (752887, 201805, 15, 1520, 201805, 15, 1520, 125464, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'May', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'ALY', '15-MAY-18 15:20:00', 'EST-5', '15-MAY-18 15:20:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'Mesonet', 68, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'NW', 'GLENHAM', 1, 'NW', 'GLENHAM', 41.53, -73.94, 41.53, -73.94, 'Severe thunderstorms impacted portions of the eastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson Valley, and Taconics during the afternoon of May 15th. Several supercell thunderstorms developed, with the lead storm resulting in a EF1 tornado near Saugerties and a swath of hail of two inches diameter and greater. Later in the afternoon, an intense thunderstorm with winds up to 105 mph resulted in a widespread area of wind damage along the I-84 corridor in southern Dutchess County. ||Over 35,000 customers in Dutchess and Ulster Counties lost power as a result of the storms. Travel restrictions were issued for southern Dutchess County due to downed trees and malfunctioning traffic lights, and Amtrak and Metro North rail service was suspended in the county. The governor deployed 125 members of the National Guard to aid storm recovery efforts.', 'A 78 mph gust was measured at the New York State Mesonet site near Beacon. Its webcam also showed a large tree downed.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (752888, 201805, 15, 1519, 201805, 15, 1519, 125464, 'NEW YORK', 36, 2018, 'May', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'ALY', '15-MAY-18 15:19:00', 'EST-5', '15-MAY-18 15:19:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'Public', 60, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'N', 'MATTEAWAN', 1, 'N', 'MATTEAWAN', 41.51, -73.97, 41.51, -73.97, 'Severe thunderstorms impacted portions of the eastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson Valley, and Taconics during the afternoon of May 15th. Several supercell thunderstorms developed, with the lead storm resulting in a EF1 tornado near Saugerties and a swath of hail of two inches diameter and greater. Later in the afternoon, an intense thunderstorm with winds up to 105 mph resulted in a widespread area of wind damage along the I-84 corridor in southern Dutchess County. ||Over 35,000 customers in Dutchess and Ulster Counties lost power as a result of the storms. Travel restrictions were issued for southern Dutchess County due to downed trees and malfunctioning traffic lights, and Amtrak and Metro North rail service was suspended in the county. The governor deployed 125 members of the National Guard to aid storm recovery efforts.', 'Large tree limbs and branches were downed in the city of Beacon.');
