produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A 79 year old female drowned in a motor vehicle on North Williams Road.  The vehicle was occupied by a single person.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784983, 201809, 14, 2012, 201809, 14, 2026, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 20:12:00', 'EST-5', '14-SEP-18 20:26:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, 'SW', 'WOODINGTON', 1, 'NE', 'LIDDELL', 35.1454, -77.6619, 35.1894, -77.8113, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down near Deep Run in association with hurricane Florence. The |damage path started along Big Oak Road where up to 5 homes sustained |roof damage. Most of this damage occurred in the direction of the storm |motion but some shingle loss was in the opposite direction. Minor tree |damage was also observed. The damage path continued northwest to Deep |Run where minor damage was found near highway 11 and along Old North |Carolina 11. This included minor shingle damage, some uprooted or |snapped trees. The damage path ended along highway 903 near but mainly |north of Liddell Road where more trees were snapped along with minor |shingle loss to some homes. The damage was sporadic and consistent with |winds in the 75 to 85 mph range due to the loss of shingles and some |uprooted trees. The time was estimated based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784984, 201809, 14, 2040, 201809, 14, 2050, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 20:40:00', 'EST-5', '14-SEP-18 20:50:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'COMBS FORK', 3, 'NW', 'PLEASANT HILL', 35.052, -77.5145, 35.1035, -77.6411, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down near Pleasant Hill in association with hurricane |Florence. The damage path started along Vine Swamps Road over the |extreme western part of Jones County. The beginning time and location |was adjusted based on radar due to the inability to access areas east of |here. Damage consisted of softwood trees uprooted in the opposite |direction of storm motion along with some minor shingle damage. More |damage was found just west of highway 258 near Zack Howard Road where a |large tree was uprooted and again in the opposite direction of the storm|motion. Most of the damage was quite sporadic on the path and was not |as concentrated as other storms surveyed thus far. Therefore the wind |speed was estimated to be between 75 and 85 mph, mainly due to the |uprooted softwood trees and minor shingle loss. The time was estimated based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (751747, 201804, 15, 1805, 201804, 15, 2245, 125355, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '15-APR-18 18:05:00', 'EST-5', '15-APR-18 22:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '35.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 2, 'SW', 'WILLOWICK', 0, 'SW', 'LAKEWOOD', 41.601, -81.5313, 41.4765, -81.8055, 'Low pressure moved northeast across northwest Ohio during the overnight hours of the 15th into the 16th. Locally heavy rainfall fell across eastern Ohio with amounts of one to two inches, with amounts over three inches in Cuyahoga County. The heavy rain over the urbanized drainage basin in Cuyahoga County resulted in urban flooding, notably in the Cleveland Heights area as well as areas in and around Akron. The heaviest rain fell over the Cuyahoga River basin which crested less than an inch from major flood stage. The major flood stage was recently raised due to extensive mitigation efforts, which kept impacts exclusive to road closures and no inundations of properties other than a few basements and garages in Valley View.||The Mahoning River basin received less rainfall than the Cuyahoga River, however stream levels had been elevated due to reservoir releases in the area. The higher river stages at the onset of the event allowed for a rapid rise at the gauge in Leavittsburg from below action to major flood stage within 24 hours. The immediately downstream station at Warren Ohio saw moderate flooding. The Killbuck Creek also rose to moderate flood stage. Several other rivers flooded in this event but only reached minor flood stage.', 'An early morning rain event on the 15th dropped a little over an inch of rain over portions of central and northern Ohio. Later that afternoon and evening a line of moderate to heavy rain moved over the same areas with an additional 1 to 2 inches with locally higher amounts in Cuyahoga County. The rainfall rates increased to around 2.5 inches per hour between 6 pm and 7 pm. Most of the flooding occurred after the second round of rain showers overwhelmed storm drainage and reached already swollen creeks and streams. Urban flooding soon developed hitting areas east of Cleveland the hardest including Euclid and Cleveland Heights. At lease three cars were submerged  in high water along Cedar Road in the Cleveland Heights area. Doan Brook and Euclid Creeks came out of their banks  but did not reach any roads.  There was a landslide at a construction site in Cleveland closing at least one lane of Interstate 77 near Broadway. Westbound lanes of Interstate 90 was closed near east 140th Street in Cleveland. Numerous local and county roads were flooded or closed during the event.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (792302, 201811, 3, 622, 201811, 3, 722, 132394, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'BOX', '03-NOV-18 06:22:00', 'EST-5', '03-NOV-18 07:22:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'SOUTH LAWRENCE', 1, 'NNW', 'SOUTH LAWRENCE', 42.6958, -71.1796, 42.6958, -71.1785, 'Low pressure over New York City early in the morning on November 3rd rapidly intensified as it moved northeastward across New England.  Heavy rain occurred in the early morning hours, with generally 1.50 to 2.50 inches in eastern sections of southern New England and up to 3.66 inches in the slopes of the Berkshires.  A few severe thunderstorms moved from Rhode Island into eastern Massachusetts around daybreak.  As the strong low passed to our north, strong to damaging westerly winds developed during the afternoon.', 'In Lawrence, there were flooded streets at Water Street and Marble Avenue.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (807259, 201812, 15, 1225, 201812, 15, 1425, 134551, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'December', 'Flood', 'LWX', '15-DEC-18 12:25:00', 'EST-5', '15-DEC-18 14:25:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'ESE', 'DUET', 2, 'E', 'PEOLA MILLS', 38.4726, -78.2525, 38.5329, -78.2332, 'A potent upper-level low and associated area of surface low pressure approached the region from southwest on the 15th. Strong forcing for ascent and ample moisture transport in advance of the upper level low led to a prolonged period of rain across the region. Eventually low pressure transferred to the coast before precipitation wound down on the 16th. Widespread rainfall totals of 2-4 inches were observed across the area.', 'VA-642 (Duet Road) closed due to high water near VA-603 (Whippoorwill Road). VA-600 Bohannon Road closed due to high water between Old Blue Ridge Turnpike and Nethers Road. VA-602 (Hughes River Road) closed due to high water near VA-231 (S F T Valley Road). VA-644 (Emmett Road) closed due to high water near VA-646 (Champe Plain Road).');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (807263, 201812, 15, 1351, 201812, 15, 1551, 134551, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'December', 'Flood', 'LWX', '15-DEC-18 13:51:00', 'EST-5', '15-DEC-18 15:51:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'W', 'MONUMENT MILLS', 0, 'WSW', 'MONUMENT MILLS', 38.5995, -78.0078, 38.5983, -78.0083, 'A potent upper-level low and associated area of surface low pressure approached the region from southwest on the 15th. Strong forcing for ascent and ample moisture transport in advance of the upper level low led to a prolonged period of rain across the region. Eventually low pressure transferred to the coast before precipitation wound down on the 16th. Widespread rainfall totals of 2-4 inches were observed across the area.', 'Hazel River Road closed near Monumental Mills Road due to high water.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (807279, 201812, 15, 2241, 201812, 16, 41, 134551, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'December', 'Flood', 'LWX', '15-DEC-18 22:41:00', 'EST-5', '16-DEC-18 00:41:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 2, 'ESE', 'CATHARPIN', 2, 'ESE', 'CATHARPIN', 38.839, -77.5401, 38.839, -77.5424, 'A potent upper-level low and associated area of surface low pressure approached the region from southwest on the 15th. Strong forcing for ascent and ample moisture transport in advance of the upper level low led to a prolonged period of rain across the region. Eventually low pressure transferred to the coast before precipitation wound down on the 16th. Widespread rainfall totals of 2-4 inches were observed across the area.', 'Featherbed Lane closed at Sudley Road as a result of flooding.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (808497, 201812, 20, 2300, 201812, 21, 100, 133712, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'December', 'Flood', 'LWX', '20-DEC-18 23:00:00', 'EST-5', '21-DEC-18 01:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'State Official', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'BRAND', 1, 'WSW', 'BRAND', 38.1133, -79.0133, 38.1141, -79.0153, 'A potent longwave trough approached the area from the west on the 20th. Warm advection and strong moisture transport ahead of the mid-upper level wave led to a prolonged period of rainfall through the day as surface low pressure tracked from the FL Panhandle to WV by evening. Rainfall totals on the order of 1-2 inches led to instances of flooding across portions of Augusta, Nelson and Albemarle Counties in VA.', 'Five to six inches of water flowing across US-250 (Jefferson Highway) between Desper Hollow Road and VA-637 (Jericho Road).');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765484, 201807, 17, 1215, 201807, 17, 1215, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 12:15:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 12:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'NNE', 'BENNETT HALL', 0, 'NNE', 'BENNETT HALL', 42.5735, -71.267, 42.5735, -71.267, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 12:15 PM EST, a thunderstorm over Billerica brought a large tree down on Salem Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765491, 201807, 17, 1228, 201807, 17, 1228, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 12:28:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 12:28:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'BILLERICA', 1, 'SSE', 'BILLERICA', 42.5418, -71.2654, 42.5418, -71.2654, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 12:28 PM EST, a thunderstorm over Billerica brought a large tree down between Friendship Street and Clinton Street.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (773636, 201807, 27, 1825, 201807, 27, 1825, 129055, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '27-JUL-18 18:25:00', 'EST-5', '27-JUL-18 18:25:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'COOP Observer', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 4, 'NNW', 'LUNENBURG', 4, 'NNW', 'LUNENBURG', 42.6549, -71.7605, 42.6549, -71.7605, 'A front remained stalled over Southern New England. South wind maintained a tropical airmass over the region.  Showers and a few thunderstorms developed in this environment.', 'At 6:25 PM EST, a tree was down across the access road in Pearl Hill State Park Campground, near Fitchburg.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (773638, 201807, 28, 2110, 201807, 28, 2110, 129056, 'RHODE ISLAND', 44, 2018, 'July', 'Lightning', 'BOX', '28-JUL-18 21:10:00', 'EST-5', '28-JUL-18 21:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '5.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'SW', 'SLOCUM', 2, 'SW', 'SLOCUM', 41.5114, -71.5563, 41.5114, -71.5563, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England late on July 28th.  This created thunderstorms that moved across Southern Rhode Island.', 'At 9:10 PM EST, lightning struck a house on Wolf Rock Road in Exeter. A large tree and wires were also brought down.  No smoke reported in house.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785009, 201809, 14, 2128, 201809, 14, 2140, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 21:28:00', 'EST-5', '14-SEP-18 21:40:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'ESE', 'KUHNS', 3, 'NNW', 'KUHNS', 34.7704, -77.0973, 34.8204, -77.1467, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down 2 miles northeast of Stella in Carteret County and White Oak township in southern Jones county. A concentrated area of snapped pine trees could be found along Highway 58 north of the Coldwater Creek neighborhood and north of Hadnot Creek. More than 25 pine trees were snapped in this area and are consistent with estimated wind speeds of around 100 mph. Along the same general path, another more concentrated area of damage was found near St. James AME Zion Church along highway 58. Here, mostly east of the highway, over 50 pine trees were snapped, and winds were estimated around 105 mph in this location. A few hardwood trees were uprooted near the end of the damage path, just over a mile farther down highway 58 north near the entrance to the Croatan National Forest. The time was estimated based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (751738, 201804, 3, 1640, 201804, 4, 1530, 125352, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '03-APR-18 16:40:00', 'EST-5', '04-APR-18 15:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '4.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'WATERFORD', 2, 'NE', 'MT LIBERTY', 40.5339, -82.6142, 40.3701, -82.599, 'A warm front lifted north out of the central plains and into Ohio on April 3 with a trailing area of low pressure over Michigan. Morning showers on April 3rd brought an inch or two of rain to southern portions of the area over the Ohio River drainage basin. Later that afternoon and evening thunderstorms moved over these same already saturated areas. This additional heavy rain resulted in rapid runoff and flooding despite rainfall totals of an inch or less. Another round of showers occurred on April 4th. The heaviest rain fell over a line from Marion to Holmes counties with one to three inches of rain reported. This rain, over saturated ground cover resulted in rapid runoff. There were numerous road closures due to flooding and even a few water rescues. The Killbuck Creek reached moderate flood stage and several others reached minor. Several other rivers reached minor flood stage.', 'Multiple rounds of heavy rain moved over Knox County on April 3 and 4th.  Rainfall measured for these two days was 2.5 to 3.5 inches with locally higher amounts. The rainfall resulted in multiple closed roads across the county. The hardest hit areas were in northern Knox County in the Fredericktown where the heaviest rain fell. A water rescue occurred on Knox Lake Road between SR 95 and Mishey Road from the Markey Run, a tributary to Knox Lake around 1020 pm on the 3rd. A vehicle was stuck in flood waters on Kildruff Road by Kokosing River in Gambier as early as 740 PM on the 3rd. This flood prone location remained flooded well into the 4th.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765503, 201807, 17, 1231, 201807, 17, 1231, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 12:31:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 12:31:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'NNE', 'SILVER LAKE', 0, 'NNE', 'SILVER LAKE', 42.572, -71.1792, 42.572, -71.1792, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 12:31 PM EST, a thunderstorm over Wilmington brought a tree down across Allston Avenue.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765505, 201807, 17, 1315, 201807, 17, 1315, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 13:15:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 13:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'SE', 'BOSTON', 1, 'SE', 'BOSTON', 42.3115, -71.0576, 42.3115, -71.0576, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 1:15 PM EST, a thunderstorm over Boston brought a tree down on Dorchester Avenue.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765508, 201807, 17, 1336, 201807, 17, 1336, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 13:36:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 13:36:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '55.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'S', 'HOCKANUM', 1, 'S', 'HOCKANUM', 42.2867, -72.6017, 42.2867, -72.6017, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 1:36 PM EST, a thunderstorm brought seven utility poles down on State Route 47 at the Hadley town line.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (781185, 201808, 8, 1830, 201808, 8, 2100, 130506, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Flash Flood', 'GYX', '08-AUG-18 18:30:00', 'EST-5', '08-AUG-18 21:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'E', 'MARTIN CORNER', 1, 'E', 'MARTIN CORNER', 43.048, -71.4347, 43.0516, -71.4274, 'An area of thunderstorms produced 2 to 4 inches of rain in less than 2 hours in portions of southern New Hampshire. This caused flooding on several streets in Concord including Hall Street, Old Suncook Road and Manchester Street where cars were flooded up to the roofs. In Hooksett, White Hall and Harvest Drive were flooded.', 'Thunderstorms produced 2 to 4 inches of rain in less than 2 hours in Hooksett which caused flooding on White Hall Street and Harvest Drive.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778656, 201808, 15, 1956, 201808, 15, 2001, 130008, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '15-AUG-18 19:56:00', 'EST-5', '15-AUG-18 20:01:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'NNE', 'TWIN MTN', 1, 'NNE', 'TWIN MTN', 44.2893, -71.543, 44.2893, -71.543, 'A cold front approaching from the northwest was the focus for late afternoon and evening convection on the 15th of August. Moderate shear with the associated shortwave increased the damaging wind potential. An isolated cell in central New Hampshire became severe and caused a couple reports of wind damage.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires on Route 3 and Parker Road in Carroll.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (783872, 201809, 15, 1300, 201809, 15, 1300, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flood', 'MHX', '15-SEP-18 13:00:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 13:00:00', 0, 0, 1, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 62, 'WSW', 'WILMAR', 60, 'WSW', 'WILMAR', 35.0404, -78.1564, 35.0512, -78.1285, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'An 81 year old male drowned in a motor vehicle on Bowden Road right along the Duplin/Sampson County line.  The vehicle was occupied by a single person.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778654, 201808, 7, 1845, 201808, 7, 1850, 130007, 'MAINE', 23, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '07-AUG-18 18:45:00', 'EST-5', '07-AUG-18 18:50:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Law Enforcement', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'ENE', 'FERNALD ARPT', 1, 'ENE', 'FERNALD ARPT', 44.6564, -68.8788, 44.6564, -68.8788, 'A very hot and humid air mass was in place over the region on the afternoon of August 7th with temperatures in the 90s and dew points in the lower 70s producing CAPE values up to 3000 J/KG. An upper trough approaching from the west brought increasing shear into the region as storms began developing in the afternoon. Multiple reports of wind damage were associated with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees on Airport Road, Monroe Road and Lebanon Road in Monroe.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778628, 201808, 3, 1430, 201808, 3, 1435, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 14:30:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 14:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'E', 'SOUTH SUTTON', 1, 'E', 'SOUTH SUTTON', 43.3195, -71.9344, 43.3195, -71.9344, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires  in South Sutton.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (749268, 201804, 3, 1646, 201804, 3, 1655, 124906, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'CLE', '03-APR-18 16:46:00', 'EST-5', '03-APR-18 16:55:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '35.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'N', 'MARION', 5, 'ENE', 'WALDO', 40.58, -83.13, 40.5037, -83.0064, 'An area of deep low pressure over southern Illinois early in the afternoon of April 3rd moved quickly northeast and crossed Lake Erie during the evening hours.  A warm front from the low lifted north across Ohio during the afternoon causing showers and thunderstorms to develop.  At least one of the thunderstorms became severe.', 'Thunderstorm winds downed several trees and large limbs across the southeastern corner of Marion County.  A home in the City of Marion was damaged by a fallen tree.  At least two vehicles were damaged by fallen trees.  Scattered power outages were reported.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (751739, 201804, 3, 1900, 201804, 4, 1500, 125352, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '03-APR-18 19:00:00', 'EST-5', '04-APR-18 15:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'N', 'EDISON', 1, 'S', 'WILLIAMSPORT', 40.57, -82.87, 40.5652, -82.7276, 'A warm front lifted north out of the central plains and into Ohio on April 3 with a trailing area of low pressure over Michigan. Morning showers on April 3rd brought an inch or two of rain to southern portions of the area over the Ohio River drainage basin. Later that afternoon and evening thunderstorms moved over these same already saturated areas. This additional heavy rain resulted in rapid runoff and flooding despite rainfall totals of an inch or less. Another round of showers occurred on April 4th. The heaviest rain fell over a line from Marion to Holmes counties with one to three inches of rain reported. This rain, over saturated ground cover resulted in rapid runoff. There were numerous road closures due to flooding and even a few water rescues. The Killbuck Creek reached moderate flood stage and several others reached minor. Several other rivers reached minor flood stage.', 'Heavy rain on April 3 and 4 measuring 2 to 3 inches resulted in numerous flooded roads across the Morrow County including the closures of RT 28,217, 126, 25, 61, 42, 172, 173.  Flooding also occurred along the Whetstone Creek near Mount Gilead.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778657, 201808, 17, 1750, 201808, 17, 1756, 130010, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 17:50:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 17:56:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'WEBB', 1, 'WSW', 'WEBB', 42.8629, -72.2444, 42.8629, -72.2444, 'A warm front was lifting north through the Connecticut Valley on the afternoon of August 17th. Clearing in the warm sector allowed for moderate CAPE values by late in the afternoon across southern New Hampshire. Several of the storms that developed produced wind damage during the evening hours.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed a tree on power lines on Flat Roof Mill Road in Swanzey.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778658, 201808, 17, 1830, 201808, 17, 1835, 130010, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 18:30:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 18:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'ESE', 'EASTVIEW', 1, 'ESE', 'EASTVIEW', 42.9252, -72.0374, 42.9252, -72.0374, 'A warm front was lifting north through the Connecticut Valley on the afternoon of August 17th. Clearing in the warm sector allowed for moderate CAPE values by late in the afternoon across southern New Hampshire. Several of the storms that developed produced wind damage during the evening hours.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed multiple trees on Cherry Hill Road in Harrisville.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778659, 201808, 17, 1835, 201808, 17, 1840, 130010, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 18:35:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 18:40:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'NNW', 'WEST PETERBOROUGH', 2, 'NNW', 'WEST PETERBOROUGH', 42.9106, -71.9813, 42.9106, -71.9813, 'A warm front was lifting north through the Connecticut Valley on the afternoon of August 17th. Clearing in the warm sector allowed for moderate CAPE values by late in the afternoon across southern New Hampshire. Several of the storms that developed produced wind damage during the evening hours.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires on Windy Row in West Peterborough.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785760, 201809, 13, 1710, 201809, 14, 15, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '13-SEP-18 17:10:00', 'EST-5', '14-SEP-18 00:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 1, 'NW', '(HAT)CAPE HATTERAS', 3, 'SSE', 'BUXTON', 35.2058, -75.7082, 35.2257, -75.5284, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Portions of NC 12 through Hatteras Village experienced water levels unsafe for travel due to a combination of heavy rain and over wash.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785762, 201809, 14, 2330, 201809, 15, 315, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 23:30:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 03:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 1, 'NW', 'NORTH TOPSAIL BEACH', 2, 'SSE', 'SWANSBORO', 34.488, -77.4317, 34.6561, -77.1106, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'County emergency services reported numerous requests for persons requesting water rescues.');
