, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Duplin County EMA reported numerous road closures throughout the county at 1353 LST on the 14th.  The town of Kenansville worked an overflow and failure at their wastewater treatment plant at 0353 LST on the 15th.  The city of Kinston was being turned into an island at 19:08 LST on the 14th. Lenoir county performed multiple water rescues throughout the county.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785778, 201809, 14, 823, 201809, 15, 445, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 08:23:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 04:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 7, 'ENE', 'WALSTONBURG', 1, 'N', 'SNOW HILL', 35.6245, -77.5864, 35.4622, -77.6819, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Flash flooding led to the closure of roads near ECU campus at 823 LST on the 14th.  Numerous reports of heavy flooding in the southern part of Greene County with most roads inaccessible by 2017 LST on the 14th.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778629, 201808, 3, 1445, 201808, 3, 1450, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 14:45:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 14:50:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'ESE', 'GILSUM', 0, 'ESE', 'GILSUM', 43.0472, -72.2626, 43.0472, -72.2626, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and produced penny size hail in Gilsum.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778630, 201808, 3, 1505, 201808, 3, 1510, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 15:05:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 15:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Utility Company', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'ESE', 'FRANKLIN', 0, 'ESE', 'FRANKLIN', 43.4268, -71.6615, 43.4268, -71.6615, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed power lines on Salisbury Road in Franklin.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778631, 201808, 3, 1510, 201808, 3, 1515, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 15:10:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 15:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'SE', 'TILTON', 1, 'SE', 'TILTON', 43.4433, -71.592, 43.4433, -71.592, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires in Tilton.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795626, 201811, 24, 2106, 201811, 24, 2306, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 21:06:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 23:06:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'State Official', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NE', 'UTICA', 2, 'NW', 'JOHNSVILLE', 39.5381, -77.385, 39.5448, -77.2511, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Frederick County 911 Center reported about thirty roads closed due to high water. The areas that had the most closures were north and east of Frederick City.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750940, 201804, 27, 1732, 201804, 27, 1732, 125212, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'CLE', '27-APR-18 17:32:00', 'EST-5', '27-APR-18 17:32:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '5.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'ESE', 'STRUTHERS', 1, 'ESE', 'STRUTHERS', 41.0658, -80.6225, 41.0658, -80.6225, 'An area of low pressure moved northeast across Ohio during the afternoon of April 27th.  An area of showers with embedded thunderstorms developed along a cold front trailing the low.  A few of the thunderstorms produced damaging wind gusts.  Most of the damage occurred in Trumbull and Mahoning Counties during the early evening.', 'Thunderstorm winds downed a tree in Struthers on Tangent Street.  The tree landed on a parked vehicle causing some damage.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750939, 201804, 27, 1732, 201804, 27, 1732, 125212, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'CLE', '27-APR-18 17:32:00', 'EST-5', '27-APR-18 17:32:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '20.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'SW', 'STRUTHERS', 2, 'SW', 'STRUTHERS', 41.0556, -80.6739, 41.0556, -80.6739, 'An area of low pressure moved northeast across Ohio during the afternoon of April 27th.  An area of showers with embedded thunderstorms developed along a cold front trailing the low.  A few of the thunderstorms produced damaging wind gusts.  Most of the damage occurred in Trumbull and Mahoning Counties during the early evening.', 'Thunderstorm wind gusts downed a tree on top of a house on the north end of Boardman along Devonshire Drive.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795590, 201811, 24, 1936, 201811, 24, 2136, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 19:36:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 21:36:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Fire Department/Rescue', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SW', 'BRIGHTON', 1, 'SW', 'BRIGHTON', 39.1934, -77.0421, 39.1926, -77.0414, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Car stranded in floodwaters on Brighton Dam Road at the Hawlings River.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795589, 201811, 24, 1912, 201811, 24, 2112, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 19:12:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 21:12:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Fire Department/Rescue', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NW', 'ROCK CREEK FOREST', 1, 'WNW', 'ROCK CREEK FOREST', 38.9873, -77.0629, 38.9855, -77.0613, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Water rescue required of people stranded by floodwaters in the Meadowbrook Park Community Center.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795595, 201811, 24, 2032, 201811, 24, 2232, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 20:32:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 22:32:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Fire Department/Rescue', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NW', 'ROCK CREEK FOREST', 1, 'N', 'ROCK CREEK FOREST', 38.9875, -77.0613, 38.9882, -77.0518, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Beach Drive closed from East West Highway to the DC line due to flooding of Rock Creek.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795658, 201811, 24, 2123, 201811, 25, 335, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 21:23:00', 'EST-5', '25-NOV-18 03:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 4, 'S', 'SAVAGE', 4, 'S', 'SAVAGE', 39.0726, -76.8397, 39.0736, -76.8352, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'The stream gage on the Little Patuxent River at Savage exceeded the flood stage of 10 feet during the indicated period. Riverside trains began to flood, and water approached fields off of Bald Eagle Drive, as well as Brock Bridge Road. The peak level of 10.59 feet occurred at 11:00 PM on the 24th.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (796564, 201811, 24, 1838, 201811, 25, 414, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 18:38:00', 'EST-5', '25-NOV-18 04:14:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 2, 'N', 'DARNESTOWN', 2, 'N', 'DARNESTOWN', 39.1275, -77.2984, 39.1255, -77.2967, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'The stream gage on the Seneca Creek at Dawsonville exceeded the 7.5 foot flood stage during the indicated period, leading to flooding of the Seneca Creek Greenway, as well as a small portion of Berryville Road west of Darnestown. The peak level of 8.18 feet occurred at 7:55 PM on the 25th.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (796565, 201811, 25, 630, 201811, 25, 1201, 132977, 'WEST VIRGINIA', 54, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '25-NOV-18 06:30:00', 'EST-5', '25-NOV-18 12:01:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 2, 'WSW', 'VANCLEVESVILLE', 1, 'WSW', 'BLAIRTON', 39.4224, -77.9395, 39.4447, -77.9381, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'The river gage on the Opequon Creek at Martinsburg exceeded the 10 foot flood stage during the indicated period, leading to flooding along portions of Douglas Grove Road (County Road 9/16) and  Bowers Road.  Additionally, water covered the stream access point at the Van Metre Ford Pedestrian Bridge. The peak level of 10.32 feet occurred at 9:30 AM on the 25th.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (752590, 201804, 16, 300, 201804, 17, 2000, 125355, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '16-APR-18 03:00:00', 'EST-5', '17-APR-18 20:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '30.00K', '0.00K', 'Fire Department/Rescue', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'WNW', 'LEAVITTSBURG', 1, 'SSE', 'WARREN', 41.2545, -80.907, 41.2112, -80.8124, 'Low pressure moved northeast across northwest Ohio during the overnight hours of the 15th into the 16th. Locally heavy rainfall fell across eastern Ohio with amounts of one to two inches, with amounts over three inches in Cuyahoga County. The heavy rain over the urbanized drainage basin in Cuyahoga County resulted in urban flooding, notably in the Cleveland Heights area as well as areas in and around Akron. The heaviest rain fell over the Cuyahoga River basin which crested less than an inch from major flood stage. The major flood stage was recently raised due to extensive mitigation efforts, which kept impacts exclusive to road closures and no inundations of properties other than a few basements and garages in Valley View.||The Mahoning River basin received less rainfall than the Cuyahoga River, however stream levels had been elevated due to reservoir releases in the area. The higher river stages at the onset of the event allowed for a rapid rise at the gauge in Leavittsburg from below action to major flood stage within 24 hours. The immediately downstream station at Warren Ohio saw moderate flooding. The Killbuck Creek also rose to moderate flood stage. Several other rivers flooded in this event but only reached minor flood stage.', 'The Mahoning River at Leavittsburg came out of banks on April 16 around 3 am and rose to major flood stage by that evening. Several homes were evacuated mainly in the Meadowbrook Drive, Dover, and 7th Street areas of Leavittsburg. The river receded back into its banks in the afternoon of the 17th. At least one home had first floor inundation with several more flooded in their garages and basements. The flooding was the result of a basin average 0.5��� on the 14th into the 15th followed by 2.5��� of rainfall later on the afternoon. Rainfall was briefly heavy during the evening of the 15th with rainfall rates around 2��� per hour. This produced rapid runoff and caused a rapid rise in the river. The river crested at 15.53 on the afternoon of the 16th, about a half foot above major flood stage. The Mahoning River at Warren reached moderate flood stage with primarily road flooding. The Eagle Creek, a tributary to the Mahoning River above Leavittsburg, reached minor flood stage.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765516, 201807, 17, 1317, 201807, 17, 1517, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 13:17:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 15:17:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'S', 'SMITH HIGHLANDS', 1, 'SSE', 'SMITH HIGHLANDS', 42.1914, -72.5983, 42.1902, -72.594, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 1:17 PM EST, the bottom of Simard Drive in Chicopee was flooded and impassable.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750950, 201804, 15, 1716, 201804, 15, 1717, 125211, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Tornado', 'CLE', '15-APR-18 17:16:00', 'EST-5', '15-APR-18 17:17:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '25.00K', '0.00K', 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'N', 'PORTAGE LAKES', 2, 'N', 'PORTAGE LAKES', 41.0131, -81.5541, 41.0137, -81.552, 'An area of low pressure moved across the Tennessee Valley on April 15th.  A front or trough stretching north from this low crossed northern Ohio causing a large area of showers to develop.  Some embedded thunderstorms were also reported.  One of the thunderstorms produced a brief tornado in Coventry in Summit County.  Some straight line downburst wind damage was also reported in nearby Twinsburg.', 'An EF1 tornado briefly touched down along Manchester Road in Coventry Township.  The initial touchdown occurred in a restaurant parking lot.  Two signs and a light pole at the restaurant were damaged.  The tornado then crossed Manchester Road and flipped a parked car at a second restaurant.  Another car nearby was spun around several times.  Several tree limbs were also snapped.  No injuries were reported.  This tornado was captured on a security camera video.  The tornado appeared to be on the ground for just over a tenth of a mile.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765518, 201807, 17, 1400, 201807, 17, 1700, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 14:00:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 17:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '5.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NW', 'WEST SPRINGFIELD', 1, 'NW', 'WEST SPRINGFIELD', 42.1088, -72.6874, 42.1087, -72.6826, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 2 PM EST,  a thunderstorm moving over Westfield and West Springfield created downpours over the area.  A car was trapped in flood waters on Old Westfield Road in West Springfield.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765481, 201807, 17, 1120, 201807, 17, 1120, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 11:20:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 11:20:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'SSW', 'WATERVILLE', 2, 'SSW', 'WATERVILLE', 42.6477, -72.0814, 42.6477, -72.0814, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 11:20 AM EST, a thunderstorm moving over Lake Dennison brought a tree down on wires on the state highway at the entrance to Lake Dennison.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765513, 201807, 17, 1710, 201807, 17, 1710, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 17:10:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 17:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '10.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'NE', 'MONTELLO', 1, 'NE', 'MONTELLO', 42.1092, -71.0111, 42.1092, -71.0111, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 5:10 PM EST, a thunderstorm near Brockton brought several trees and wires down on Winter Street.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (773618, 201807, 17, 1550, 201807, 17, 1800, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 15:50:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 18:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 6, 'E', 'FAYVILLE', 6, 'E', 'FAYVILLE', 42.2987, -71.4103, 42.2987, -71.4079, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 3:50 PM EST, heavy rain brought one foot of flooding at the intersection of state route 9 and state route 126 in Framingham.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795629, 201811, 24, 2106, 201811, 24, 2306, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 21:06:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 23:06:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Fire Department/Rescue', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SE', 'MT ZION', 1, 'SE', 'MT ZION', 39.17, -77.09, 39.1696, -77.0873, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Car stranded in high water near the intersection of Volunteer Drive and Olney-Laytonsville Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (795637, 201811, 24, 2334, 201811, 25, 134, 132975, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 23:34:00', 'EST-5', '25-NOV-18 01:34:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'N', 'COLUMBIA', 1, 'N', 'COLUMBIA', 39.24, -76.85, 39.2385, -76.8493, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Road closed due to flooding at intersection of Route 108 and Woodland Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (796610, 201811, 24, 1745, 201811, 24, 1945, 132976, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'November', 'Flood', 'LWX', '24-NOV-18 17:45:00', 'EST-5', '24-NOV-18 19:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Law Enforcement', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NE', 'CENTREVILLE', 1, 'NE', 'CENTREVILLE', 38.8393, -77.4058, 38.8406, -77.4059, 'A negatively tilted trough approached the area on the 24th as surface low pressure tracked up toward the Great Lakes and secondary low pressure formed along the Carolina Coast. Lift ahead of the negatively tilted trough/within the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak led to a period of moderate to heavy rain through much of the day on the 24th. Rainfall totals were generally between 1-2 inches across the area, resulting in many instances of flooding.', 'Both eastbound lanes of Lee Highway were closed at Stringfellow Road due to floodwaters.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784985, 201809, 15, 200, 201809, 15, 205, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '15-SEP-18 02:00:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 02:05:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'SSW', 'WILLIS LNDG', 2, 'WNW', 'WILLIS LNDG', 34.6243, -77.2362, 34.6595, -77.251, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down in Camp Lejeune. Maximum winds were estimated at around 90 mph. The damage path started along Highway 172, south of Lyman Road. Most of the damage was confined to about 10 to 15 snapped pine trees with a few uprooted pine trees. Most of this damage was oriented toward the southwest or west. However, a small building that was open toward the west, had damage inside the building pointed toward the southeast or in the opposite direction of the storm motion. We were not able to examine the total path, but more damage was found to the northwest of the initial point crossing Lyman Road. The time was based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765425, 201807, 6, 1120, 201807, 6, 1220, 127647, 'RHODE ISLAND', 44, 2018, 'July', 'Flood', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:20:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 12:20:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '5.00K', '0.00K', 'Trained Spotter', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'NNW', 'ABBOTT RUN', 0, 'NE', 'ABBOTT RUN', 41.9529, -71.4015, 41.9533, -71.3945, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th. This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:20 AM EST, a trained spotter reported flooding from heavy rain on Industrial Road in Cumberland.  People on the road were unable to exit their vehicles.  Rainfall of 1.25 inches was reported in Cumberland.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (752591, 201804, 15, 1800, 201804, 16, 300, 125355, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '15-APR-18 18:00:00', 'EST-5', '16-APR-18 03:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'WNW', 'LATTASBURG', 1, 'E', 'FUNK', 40.8825, -82.1058, 40.7492, -82.0916, 'Low pressure moved northeast across northwest Ohio during the overnight hours of the 15th into the 16th. Locally heavy rainfall fell across eastern Ohio with amounts of one to two inches, with amounts over three inches in Cuyahoga County. The heavy rain over the urbanized drainage basin in Cuyahoga County resulted in urban flooding, notably in the Cleveland Heights area as well as areas in and around Akron. The heaviest rain fell over the Cuyahoga River basin which crested less than an inch from major flood stage. The major flood stage was recently raised due to extensive mitigation efforts, which kept impacts exclusive to road closures and no inundations of properties other than a few basements and garages in Valley View.||The Mahoning River basin received less rainfall than the Cuyahoga River, however stream levels had been elevated due to reservoir releases in the area. The higher river stages at the onset of the event allowed for a rapid rise at the gauge in Leavittsburg from below action to major flood stage within 24 hours. The immediately downstream station at Warren Ohio saw moderate flooding. The Killbuck Creek also rose to moderate flood stage. Several other rivers flooded in this event but only reached minor flood stage.', 'Over a dozen roads were flooded after heavy rain moved over portions of Wayne County on the evening of the 15th. Between 0.5-1.0 of rain fell on the night of the 14th into the 15th, with a second round of heavier rain that afternoon and evening. The second round of rain produced around 2 to 2.5 of additional rain. The cumulative runoff from this rain caused flooding of several roads across the county.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765519, 201807, 17, 1409, 201807, 17, 1700, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 14:09:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 17:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'INGLESIDE', 1, 'SSE', 'INGLESIDE', 42.1669, -72.6225, 42.1673, -72.6229, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 2:09 PM EST, Meeting House Road in Chicopee was flooded and impassable.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765521, 201807, 17, 1410, 201807, 17, 1710, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 14:10:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 17:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '5.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 2, 'WNW', 'WEST SPRINGFIELD', 2, 'WNW', 'WEST SPRINGFIELD', 42.1067, -72.7026, 42.109, -72.7016, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 2:10 PM EST, heavy rain from a thunderstorm flooded and closed U.S.Route 20 in Westfield at the intersection with East Mountain Road.  A car was trapped in the flood waters.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765525, 201807, 17, 1455, 201807, 17, 1800, 127649, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flash Flood', 'BOX', '17-JUL-18 14:55:00', 'EST-5', '17-JUL-18 18:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SSW', 'WORCESTER', 1, 'SSW', 'WORCESTER', 42.2601, -71.8052, 42.2602, -71.806, 'A cold front moved east from the Great Lakes, crossing Massachusetts during the night of the 17th.  The thunderstorms brought strong wind gusts and wind damage during the afternoon and evening.  Thunderstorms also brought downpours in Western, Central, and Northeast Massachusetts. Two to four inches of rain fell from Westfield through Worcester to Boston.', 'At 2:55 PM EST, heavy rain in Worcester brought street flooding to a depth of one foot at the intersection of Chandler Street and Murray Avenue.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778663, 201808, 29, 1608, 201808, 29, 1613, 130012, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '29-AUG-18 16:08:00', 'EST-5', '29-AUG-18 16:13:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Social Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'WEST STEWARTSTOWN', 1, 'NNW', 'WEST STEWARTSTOWN', 44.9945, -71.532, 44.9945, -71.532, 'A hot and humid air mass was in place across the region on the afternoon of August 29th as a cold front approached from the northwest. Convection developed ahead of this boundary as it headed east. A few storm cells produced wind damage as they pushed into far northern New Hampshire.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed large limbs in West Stewartstown.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778665, 201808, 29, 1614, 201808, 29, 1619, 130012, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '29-AUG-18 16:14:00', 'EST-5', '29-AUG-18 16:19:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Utility Company', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 4, 'NE', 'PITTSBURG', 4, 'NE', 'PITTSBURG', 45.0878, -71.3487, 45.0878, -71.3487, 'A hot and humid air mass was in place across the region on the afternoon of August 29th as a cold front approached from the northwest. Convection developed ahead of this boundary as it headed east. A few storm cells produced wind damage as they pushed into far northern New Hampshire.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed a tree on wires on Macneil Lane in Pittsburg.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765411, 201807, 6, 1126, 201807, 6, 1500, 127646, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Flood', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:26:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 15:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '10.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'SSE', 'RANDOLPH', 0, 'SSE', 'RANDOLPH', 42.1654, -71.0468, 42.1654, -71.0466, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th.  This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:26 AM EST, heavy rain caused street flooding in Randolph at the intersection of Warren Street and Highland Avenue.  Multiple cars were trapped in the flooding.  Rainfall of 1.90 inches was reported in nearby Stoughton Massachusetts.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765412, 201807, 6, 1135, 201807, 6, 1135, 127646, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Lightning', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:35:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 11:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '10.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'FOXVALE', 1, 'WSW', 'FOXVALE', 42.0455, -71.2494, 42.0455, -71.2494, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th.  This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:35 AM EST, lightning from a thunderstorm over Foxboro started a house fire on High Street.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765414, 201807, 6, 1135, 201807, 6, 1135, 127646, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Lightning', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:35:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 11:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '10.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'HOLBROOK', 1, 'WSW', 'HOLBROOK', 42.1438, -71.0176, 42.1438, -71.0176, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th.  This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:35 AM EST, lightning from a thunderstorm over Holbrook started a house fire on East Shore Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765417, 201807, 6, 1136, 201807, 6, 1136, 127646, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Lightning', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:36:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 11:36:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '10.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'NORTH WEYMOUTH', 1, 'NNW', 'NORTH WEYMOUTH', 42.2418, -70.9581, 42.2418, -70.9581, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th.  This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:36 AM EST, lightning from a thunderstorm over Weymouth started an attic fire on Rosemont Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (765421, 201807, 6, 1131, 201807, 6, 1131, 127646, 'MASSACHUSETTS', 25, 2018, 'July', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'BOX', '06-JUL-18 11:31:00', 'EST-5', '06-JUL-18 11:31:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 45, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'SSW', 'KENT PARK', 1, 'SSW', 'KENT PARK', 42.0825, -70.6938, 42.0825, -70.6938, 'A cold front moved through Southern New England on July 6th.  This caused numerous thunderstorms, some with locally heavy rainfall.', 'At 11:31 AM EST, a thunderstorm downburst brought down a tree on Webster Street in Marshfield.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778633, 201808, 3, 1535, 201808, 3, 1539, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 15:35:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 15:39:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, '0.00K', 'NWS Storm Survey', 70, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'NNE', 'GILMANTON', 2, 'NNE', 'GILMANTON', 43.4502, -71.4051, 43.4502, -71.4051, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm  produced a microburst in the area immediately around Sawyer Lake in Gilmanton with the greatest observed damage along Woods Road on the west  side of the lake.  Numerous large trees were snapped or uprooted by the microburst with several homes or cottages damaged by falling trees.  Residents in the area reported that very heavy rain and nickel-sized hail accompanied the strong winds.  The area damaged by the microburst was estimated to be 1100 yards long and 500 yards with maximum wind gusts of 80 mph.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (751751, 201804, 16, 0, 201804, 17, 200, 125355, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Flood', 'CLE', '16-APR-18 00:00:00', 'EST-5', '17-APR-18 02:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '3.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'N', 'VALLEY VIEW', 2, 'E', 'ALEXANDERS', 41.4, -81.6, 41.3685, -81.5779, 'Low pressure moved northeast across northwest Ohio during the overnight hours of the 15th into the 16th. Locally heavy rainfall fell across eastern Ohio with amounts of one to two inches, with amounts over three inches in Cuyahoga County. The heavy rain over the urbanized drainage basin in Cuyahoga County resulted in urban flooding, notably in the Cleveland Heights area as well as areas in and around Akron. The heaviest rain fell over the Cuyahoga River basin which crested less than an inch from major flood stage. The major flood stage was recently raised due to extensive mitigation efforts, which kept impacts exclusive to road closures and no inundations of properties other than a few basements and garages in Valley View.||The Mahoning River basin received less rainfall than the Cuyahoga River, however stream levels had been elevated due to reservoir releases in the area. The higher river stages at the onset of the event allowed for a rapid rise at the gauge in Leavittsburg from below action to major flood stage within 24 hours. The immediately downstream station at Warren Ohio saw moderate flooding. The Killbuck Creek also rose to moderate flood stage. Several other rivers flooded in this event but only reached minor flood stage.', 'The Cuyahoga River at Independence, a river forecast site, reach moderate flood stage. The river reached flood stage around 8 pm on the 15th, exceeded moderate flood stage a few hours later, and crested at 20.9 feet at 6 am on the 1th. The river came out of banks flooding roads in the Valley View community. Some garages and basements were flooded, but damage was limited. The major flood stage, 21.0 feet, was recently raised due to extensive mitigation efforts which were validated during this event.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (781191, 201808, 17, 1920, 201808, 17, 2200, 130507, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Flash Flood', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 19:20:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 22:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '50.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'SSE', 'DUBLIN', 1, 'S', 'DUBLIN', 42.9138, -72.0668, 42.9038, -72.0658, 'An area of thunderstorms produced 2.5 to 4 inches of rain in less than 3 hours across portions of southwest New Hampshire. This caused flooding of streets and homes along with several road washouts. In Dublin, Dublin Road and Beech Hill Road were washed out and Route 101 was flooded. Homes  on Main Street were flooded resulting in evacuations. In Harrisville, Cherry Hill Road and Hancock Road washed out and a vehicle was flooded.', 'Thunderstorms produced 2.5 to 4 inches of rain in less than 3 hours which caused flooding and road washouts in Dublin. Beech Hill Road and Dublin Road were washed out. Homes were flooded on Main Street (route 101), and residents were evacuated.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (781205, 201808, 17, 1920, 201808, 17, 2200, 130507, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Flash Flood', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 19:20:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 22:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '35.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'ENE', 'EASTVIEW', 1, 'E', 'EASTVIEW', 42.9328, -72.0347, 42.9307, -72.0384, 'An area of thunderstorms produced 2.5 to 4 inches of rain in less than 3 hours across portions of southwest New Hampshire. This caused flooding of streets and homes along with several road washouts. In Dublin, Dublin Road and Beech Hill Road were washed out and Route 101 was flooded. Homes  on Main Street were flooded resulting in evacuations. In Harrisville, Cherry Hill Road and Hancock Road washed out and a vehicle was flooded.', 'Thunderstorms produced 2.5 to 4 inches of rain in less than 3 hours which caused flooding in Harrisville. Cherry Hill Road and Hancock Road washed out and a vehicle was flooded.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (782826, 201803, 1, 1500, 201803, 1, 1600, 122352, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'March', 'Flood', 'ILN', '01-MAR-18 15:00:00', 'EST-5', '01-MAR-18 16:00:00', 0, 0, 1, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'MASSIEVILLE', 1, 'WSW', 'MASSIEVILLE', 39.2656, -82.9926, 39.2651, -82.9967, 'Showers and isolated thunderstorms developed during the afternoon ahead of a cold front that was moving through the Ohio Valley.', 'Trego Creek was running high due to recent heavy rains. An 8 year old boy drowned after being swept away in the creek. M8IW.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785783, 201809, 14, 2005, 201809, 15, 130, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 20:05:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 01:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 2, 'SW', 'LIMA', 2, 'ENE', 'MIDWAY', 35.1798, -77.1717, 35.1265, -77.1933, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Beaman road is flooded and impassable near NC-55.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785785, 201809, 14, 2045, 201809, 15, 145, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 20:45:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 01:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 1, 'WSW', 'WASHINGTON ARPT', 0, 'SSW', 'CHOCOWINITY', 35.5609, -77.0739, 35.514, -77.1037, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'The city of Washington remained under a curfew due to numerous streets that were flooded.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (785789, 201809, 15, 600, 201809, 15, 1400, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '15-SEP-18 06:00:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 14:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 1, 'NW', 'WARSAW', 2, 'SSW', 'ROSE HILL', 35.0154, -78.0934, 34.7952, -78.0432, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'The Warsaw FD reported 20 inches of rainfall at 0500 LST on the 15th.  Duplin county experienced catastrophic flash flooding with numerous rescues being performed with emergency evacuations in place as of 0651 LST.  Flash flooding has combined with river flooding and land locked all fire departments and rescue teams, with multiple water rescues still being reported as of 0815 LST.  At 1136 LST around 110 roads were washed out by flash flooding with inland river flooding not allowing for evacuation travel.');
