hes, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'State Route 1226 (New Liberty Road) closed near Clarks Road due to flooding.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784977, 201809, 13, 1004, 201809, 13, 1013, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '13-SEP-18 10:04:00', 'EST-5', '13-SEP-18 10:13:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'E', 'SWINDELL', 2, 'SW', 'OYSTER CREEK LNDG', 35.3998, -76.2894, 35.3854, -76.343, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'NWS storm survey team confirmed the tornado occurred near Swan Quarter. The damage path started near Farrow Rd, and consisted of a large softwood that was snapped. More softwoods were snapped along with some hardwoods being uprooted along Oyster Creek St. A large waterspout was reported off the ferry terminal at Swan Quarter by the ferrymaster. The end of the pathw was an end over the western part of the Pamlico Sound based on the eyewitness account. Due to less than 10 trees being snapped and only a few hardwoods uprooted, maximum estimated winds were at the lower end of the scale or around 90 mph. The time was based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784979, 201809, 13, 1851, 201809, 13, 1855, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '13-SEP-18 18:51:00', 'EST-5', '13-SEP-18 18:55:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, 'E', 'CHERRY PT LNDG', 2, 'ESE', 'CHERRY PT LNDG', 34.925, -76.806, 34.9197, -76.8137, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down during the early evening near Havelock.  A large swath of snapped pine trees were found in the Cherry Branch neighborhood.  The damage was most extensive from near the dead end of Seattle Slew Drive southwest toward the intersection of Route 306 and Pine Cliff Road.  Over 100 pine trees were snapped in this general area, with a few pine and hardwood trees uprooted near both ends of the damage path. Maximum estimated winds were 105 mph. The time was based on radar.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (784978, 201809, 13, 1023, 201809, 13, 1026, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Tornado', 'MHX', '13-SEP-18 10:23:00', 'EST-5', '13-SEP-18 10:26:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'NWS Storm Survey', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'N', 'LOWLAND', 1, 'WNW', 'LOWLAND', 35.3215, -76.5837, 35.3052, -76.594, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'A tornado touched down near Lowland.  The damage path started toward the north end of Fulford Point Road and consisted of numerous softwood trees snapped, some hardwoods that were uprooted and a utility pole that was destroyed.  The path continued to the intersection of Lowland Road and Fulford Point Road where numerous trees were blow downs and softwood trees were snapped.  The damage path may have continued farther to the southwest but was not accessible to us.  With less than 10 softwoods snapped in any given area the maximum winds were kept on the lower end of the scale or around 90 MPH.  The time was based on radar data.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778632, 201808, 3, 1517, 201808, 3, 1522, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 15:17:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 15:22:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'WNW', 'BELMONT', 2, 'WNW', 'BELMONT', 43.4434, -71.5123, 43.4434, -71.5123, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires on Clark Street in Belmont.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (750953, 201804, 15, 1733, 201804, 15, 1733, 125211, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'April', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'CLE', '15-APR-18 17:33:00', 'EST-5', '15-APR-18 17:33:00', 1, 0, 0, 0, '100.00K', '0.00K', 'NWS Storm Survey', 61, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'ESE', 'MACEDONIA', 2, 'ESE', 'MACEDONIA', 41.3061, -81.4669, 41.3061, -81.4669, 'An area of low pressure moved across the Tennessee Valley on April 15th.  A front or trough stretching north from this low crossed northern Ohio causing a large area of showers to develop.  Some embedded thunderstorms were also reported.  One of the thunderstorms produced a brief tornado in Coventry in Summit County.  Some straight line downburst wind damage was also reported in nearby Twinsburg.', 'Thunderstorm downburst winds estimated to be around 70 mph damaged the roof of a large warehouse on Independence Parkway in Twinsburg.  There was one minor injury.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (786290, 201809, 17, 410, 201809, 17, 510, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '17-SEP-18 04:10:00', 'EST-5', '17-SEP-18 05:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 2, 'WNW', 'FAISON', 2, 'SW', 'FAISON', 35.1311, -78.1679, 35.0934, -78.1537, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Partial lane washout between US117 and State Route 1332 in Faison.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (786894, 201809, 14, 937, 201809, 15, 330, 130979, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'September', 'Flash Flood', 'MHX', '14-SEP-18 09:37:00', 'EST-5', '15-SEP-18 03:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain / Tropical System', NULL, 1, 'W', 'MAYSVILLE', 3, 'NE', 'TRENTON', 34.901, -77.2541, 35.106, -77.3377, 'Hurricane Florence was a long-lived Cape Verde hurricane and the wettest tropical cyclone on record in the Carolinas. The sixth named storm, third hurricane, and the first major hurricane of the 2018 Atlantic hurricane season, Florence originated from a strong tropical wave that emerged off the west coast of Africa on August 30, 2018. Florence became a tropical depression near Cape Verde on August 31 and progressed west-northwest, becoming a Tropical Storm on September 1. Florence strengthened rapidly on September 4���5, becoming a Category 4 storm on the Saffir-Simpson wind scale with maximum sustained winds of 130 mph. Florence weakened to a tropical storm by September 7, but the system regained hurricane strength on September 9 and major hurricane status with winds of 140 mph on September 10. However, increasing wind shear caused the storm''s winds to gradually weaken over the next few days. However, the storm''s wind field continued to grow. By the evening of September 13, Florence had been downgraded to a Category 1 hurricane. Hurricane Florence made landfall near Wrightsville Beach early on Saturday September 15, and weakened further as it slowly moved inland.||Florence produced extensive wind damage along the North Carolina coast from Cape Lookout, across Carteret, Onslow, Pender and New Hanover counties. Thousands of downed trees caused widespread power outages to nearly all of eastern North Carolina. The historic legacy of Hurricane Florence will be record breaking storm surge of 9 to 13 feet and widespread devastating rainfall of 20 to 30 inches, locally up to 36 inches, which produced catastrophic and life-threatening flooding. The hardest hit areas included New Bern, Newport, Belhaven, Oriental, North Topsail Beach and Jacksonville, along with Downeast Carteret County, or basically south of a line from Kinston to Cedar Island. A storm total rainfall of 34.00 inches was reported in Swansboro, while the NWS office in Newport recorded 25.20 inches.  Wind gusts of 106 mph were reported at Cape Lookout with 105 mph at Fort Macon.', 'Called Jones/Lenoir 911 center and they reported numerous roads impassable across both counties.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778636, 201808, 3, 1525, 201808, 3, 1530, 130004, 'NEW HAMPSHIRE', 33, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '03-AUG-18 15:25:00', 'EST-5', '03-AUG-18 15:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 0, 'WSW', 'BELMONT', 0, 'WSW', 'BELMONT', 43.4277, -71.4888, 43.4277, -71.4888, 'A warm front was draped across central New Hampshire into the foothills of western Maine on the morning of August 3rd. South of this boundary, a warm and very humid air mass was in place and convection began developing by late morning in the unstable warm sector. Due to the high precipitable water values, several of these storms produced wet micro bursts and damaging winds along with a few reports of hail. Some isolated flash flooding also occurred with these storms.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed multiple trees and wires at 208 Shaker Road in Belmont.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778662, 201808, 17, 2211, 201808, 17, 2215, 130011, 'MAINE', 23, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 22:11:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 22:15:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'SW', 'NORTH AUGUSTA', 2, 'SW', 'NORTH AUGUSTA', 44.3524, -69.801, 44.3524, -69.801, 'A warm front lifting north through western Maine was the focus for showers and thunderstorms during the evening of August 17th. A few of these storms produced wind damage in south central Maine during the late evening hours.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees and wires on Leighton Road in Augusta.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778660, 201808, 17, 2132, 201808, 17, 2136, 130011, 'MAINE', 23, 2018, 'August', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'GYX', '17-AUG-18 21:32:00', 'EST-5', '17-AUG-18 21:36:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Broadcast Media', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'W', 'FAIR GROUNDS', 1, 'W', 'FAIR GROUNDS', 44.1209, -70.2505, 44.1209, -70.2505, 'A warm front lifting north through western Maine was the focus for showers and thunderstorms during the evening of August 17th. A few of these storms produced wind damage in south central Maine during the late evening hours.', 'A severe thunderstorm downed trees on Summer Street in Auburn.');
