re a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'Numerous trees were blown down by severe straight-line winds along Charlie Brown Lane.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (789956, 201810, 11, 1500, 201810, 11, 1600, 130716, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 15:00:00', 'EST-5', '11-OCT-18 16:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '30.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', 60, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 3, 'NW', 'SAXE', 2, 'ESE', 'ABILENE', 36.9586, -78.7203, 37.1199, -78.5244, 'Hurricane Michael made landfall along the Florida panhandle as major hurricane (Category 4 on the Saffir-Simpson scale) on Wednesday afternoon, October 10th, 2018, then tracked northeastward with the northern portion of the storm circulation tracking across portions of Southside, Virginia Thursday afternoon, the 11th. A deep flow of tropical moisture into the Appalachians and central mid Atlantic region ahead of the storm began to generate excessive rainfall across portions of the region as early as Wednesday evening, mainly along the Interstate 77 corridor, causing some degree of flash flooding and otherwise saturating the ground even before the arrival of the rainfall directly associated with Michael.||As the storm circulation approached on Thursday, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall especially east of Interstate 81. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, mainly from the mountains of North Carolina up through Southside Virginia. This resulted in significant flash flooding with flash flood emergencies issued for the city of Roanoke, as well as Roanoke County, and southern Pittsylvania county including the city of Danville.  Unfortunately, there were five fatalities as vehicles were swept away by flash flood waters in Pittsylvania County, Danville, and Charlotte County, Virginia.||The massive volume of water also then generated rapid rises on rivers including the New, Roanoke, and Dan. Major flooding was reported at several river gage locations along with a couple of record crests. There were a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'At least twenty trees, as well as several power lines, were blown down across portions of Charlotte County as very heavy rainfall associated with thunderstorms which developed in one of the feeder bands of Tropical Storm Michael allowed strong winds aloft to mix down to the surface.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (789952, 201810, 11, 1550, 201810, 11, 1640, 130716, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 15:50:00', 'EST-5', '11-OCT-18 16:40:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '8.00K', '0.00K', 'Emergency Manager', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 6, 'W', 'MT RUSH', 2, 'E', 'ALPHA', 37.5341, -78.723, 37.5848, -78.3827, 'Hurricane Michael made landfall along the Florida panhandle as major hurricane (Category 4 on the Saffir-Simpson scale) on Wednesday afternoon, October 10th, 2018, then tracked northeastward with the northern portion of the storm circulation tracking across portions of Southside, Virginia Thursday afternoon, the 11th. A deep flow of tropical moisture into the Appalachians and central mid Atlantic region ahead of the storm began to generate excessive rainfall across portions of the region as early as Wednesday evening, mainly along the Interstate 77 corridor, causing some degree of flash flooding and otherwise saturating the ground even before the arrival of the rainfall directly associated with Michael.||As the storm circulation approached on Thursday, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall especially east of Interstate 81. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, mainly from the mountains of North Carolina up through Southside Virginia. This resulted in significant flash flooding with flash flood emergencies issued for the city of Roanoke, as well as Roanoke County, and southern Pittsylvania county including the city of Danville.  Unfortunately, there were five fatalities as vehicles were swept away by flash flood waters in Pittsylvania County, Danville, and Charlotte County, Virginia.||The massive volume of water also then generated rapid rises on rivers including the New, Roanoke, and Dan. Major flooding was reported at several river gage locations along with a couple of record crests. There were a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'At least fifteen trees were blown down across portions of Buckingham County as very heavy rainfall associated with thunderstorms which developed in one of the feeder bands of Tropical Storm Michael allowed strong winds aloft to mix down to the surface.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (791173, 201810, 11, 900, 201810, 11, 1500, 131036, 'NORTH CAROLINA', 37, 2018, 'October', 'Flash Flood', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 09:00:00', 'EST-5', '11-OCT-18 15:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Newspaper', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'FERGUSON', 1, 'SE', 'HALLS MILLS', 36.1127, -81.3776, 36.2912, -81.2063, 'As the direct storm circulation from Michael approached in the early morning hours of October 11, rainfall and strong winds began first in Watauga County in northwest NC, and progressed north and east through the day.  In addition, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall, especially over the eastern counties.  Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, from the mountains of North Carolina across the foothills and piedmont.  Much of the rain fell in a 3 to 6 hour period,  resulting in widespread, significant flash flooding and river flooding in all the NC counties of the Blacksburg WFO area of responsibility.  In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Wind speeds were observed strongest across the higher terrain from the Blue Ridge westward, where 40 to 50 mph gusts were observed.  Winds speeds further east were generally lower as the storm continued to weaken along its eastward track.  Many trees and power lines were blown down in the water logged soil across the northern portion of North Carolina, resulting in blocked roads and numerous power outages. The state of North Carolina included the following counties located within the Blacksburg WFO area in north central NC in a request (December 12, 2018) for a federal disaster declaration as a result of Tropical Storm Michael: Caswell, Rockingham, Stokes, Surry and Yadkin. The outcome of the request was not available at this writing.', 'Bands of heavy rainfall from Tropical Storm Michael began rotating across the county in the mid-morning and amounts from 2 to 4 inches in several hours were observed. According to later news reports several businesses on 10th Street in North Wilkesboro had water enter their buildings. Cub Creek flooded a portion of Cub Creek Park in Wilkesboro. Flooding was also reported in the Roaring River community where part of the road between NC 268 East and the bridge over the Yadkin was flooded. The Reddies River at North Wilkesboro (REDN7) contributed to the flooding and crested at its highest stage (11.35 feet) since November, 1989 when it reached 11.72 feet.  Several bridges across the upper Reddies were flooded.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (788634, 201810, 11, 110, 201810, 12, 1640, 130716, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 01:10:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 16:40:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'WNW', 'GLEN LYN', 0, 'E', 'NARROWS', 37.3724, -80.8676, 37.3308, -80.8141, 'Hurricane Michael made landfall along the Florida panhandle as major hurricane (Category 4 on the Saffir-Simpson scale) on Wednesday afternoon, October 10th, 2018, then tracked northeastward with the northern portion of the storm circulation tracking across portions of Southside, Virginia Thursday afternoon, the 11th. A deep flow of tropical moisture into the Appalachians and central mid Atlantic region ahead of the storm began to generate excessive rainfall across portions of the region as early as Wednesday evening, mainly along the Interstate 77 corridor, causing some degree of flash flooding and otherwise saturating the ground even before the arrival of the rainfall directly associated with Michael.||As the storm circulation approached on Thursday, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall especially east of Interstate 81. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, mainly from the mountains of North Carolina up through Southside Virginia. This resulted in significant flash flooding with flash flood emergencies issued for the city of Roanoke, as well as Roanoke County, and southern Pittsylvania county including the city of Danville.  Unfortunately, there were five fatalities as vehicles were swept away by flash flood waters in Pittsylvania County, Danville, and Charlotte County, Virginia.||The massive volume of water also then generated rapid rises on rivers including the New, Roanoke, and Dan. Major flooding was reported at several river gage locations along with a couple of record crests. There were a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'The New River at Glen Lyn (GLLV2) crested at 19.47 feet (121000 cfs) on the morning of the 12th. Major flood stage is 18 feet. It was the 3rd highest reading at this location since USGS records began in 1940 and the highest since January 16, 1995 (19.83 feet). In terms of flood frequency it fell near a 25-year event (0.04 annual chance flood). There were considerable impacts along the New River including several mobile home parks flooded.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (788673, 201810, 11, 415, 201810, 12, 545, 130716, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 04:15:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 05:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 0, 'SE', 'STAFFORDSVILLE', 0, 'ESE', 'BANE', 37.2461, -80.7127, 37.2686, -80.7141, 'Hurricane Michael made landfall along the Florida panhandle as major hurricane (Category 4 on the Saffir-Simpson scale) on Wednesday afternoon, October 10th, 2018, then tracked northeastward with the northern portion of the storm circulation tracking across portions of Southside, Virginia Thursday afternoon, the 11th. A deep flow of tropical moisture into the Appalachians and central mid Atlantic region ahead of the storm began to generate excessive rainfall across portions of the region as early as Wednesday evening, mainly along the Interstate 77 corridor, causing some degree of flash flooding and otherwise saturating the ground even before the arrival of the rainfall directly associated with Michael.||As the storm circulation approached on Thursday, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall especially east of Interstate 81. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, mainly from the mountains of North Carolina up through Southside Virginia. This resulted in significant flash flooding with flash flood emergencies issued for the city of Roanoke, as well as Roanoke County, and southern Pittsylvania county including the city of Danville.  Unfortunately, there were five fatalities as vehicles were swept away by flash flood waters in Pittsylvania County, Danville, and Charlotte County, Virginia.||The massive volume of water also then generated rapid rises on rivers including the New, Roanoke, and Dan. Major flooding was reported at several river gage locations along with a couple of record crests. There were a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'Water was reported over Route 100 due to flooding from Walker Creek. The gage at Bane (BANV2) briefly touched minor flood stage of 11 feet (7,800 cfs) very early on the 11th in response to the PRE (Predecessor Rainfall Event) and then had a more significant second rise from the rains directly associated with Michael, reaching a crest of 13.46 (12,000 cfs) feet early on the 12th. Moderate flood stage is 14 feet.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790964, 201810, 26, 1505, 201810, 26, 1505, 132194, 'GULF OF MEXICO', 85, 2018, 'October', 'Waterspout', 'KEY', '26-OCT-18 15:05:00', 'EST-5', '26-OCT-18 15:05:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, 'WNW', 'DUCK KEY', 7, 'WNW', 'DUCK KEY', 24.81, -81.01, 24.81, -81.01, 'Waterspouts were observed in association with rapidly-developing showers in advance of a cold front near the Middle Florida Keys.', 'Several waterspouts were observed about 8 miles north of Marathon.  Two of the waterspouts had a visible spray ring.  The reports were relayed via social media.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778386, 201810, 2, 2156, 201810, 2, 2156, 129938, 'NEW JERSEY', 34, 2018, 'October', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'PHI', '02-OCT-18 21:56:00', 'EST-5', '02-OCT-18 21:56:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 26, 'NNW', 'HAMMONTON', 26, 'NNW', 'HAMMONTON', 39.9561, -75.0581, 39.9561, -75.0581, 'Supercellular severe thunderstorms caused 2 tornadoes, wind damage, and hail across the region all part of a record breaking tornado outbreak across Pennsylvania.', 'Public reports tree down in front yard.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (779958, 201808, 12, 1740, 201808, 12, 1740, 130272, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'August', 'Hail', 'PBZ', '12-AUG-18 17:40:00', 'EST-5', '12-AUG-18 17:40:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 0.88, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'N', 'NORWICH', 1, 'N', 'NORWICH', 39.99, -81.8, 39.99, -81.8, 'Upper level low pressure was in place across the upper Ohio Valley on the afternoon of the 12th.  A shortwave rotating around the upper low worked with existing instability to produce scattered thunderstorms in Ohio, with one storm in Muskingum County becoming strong enough to produce large hail.', 'Citizens of Muskingum County reported hail.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (779960, 201808, 12, 1745, 201808, 12, 1745, 130272, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'August', 'Hail', 'PBZ', '12-AUG-18 17:45:00', 'EST-5', '12-AUG-18 17:45:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'N', 'NORWICH', 0, 'N', 'NORWICH', 39.98, -81.8, 39.98, -81.8, 'Upper level low pressure was in place across the upper Ohio Valley on the afternoon of the 12th.  A shortwave rotating around the upper low worked with existing instability to produce scattered thunderstorms in Ohio, with one storm in Muskingum County becoming strong enough to produce large hail.', 'Citizens of Muskingum County reported hail.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (779961, 201808, 12, 1749, 201808, 12, 1749, 130272, 'OHIO', 39, 2018, 'August', 'Hail', 'PBZ', '12-AUG-18 17:49:00', 'EST-5', '12-AUG-18 17:49:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 0.75, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'SE', 'NORWICH', 1, 'SE', 'NORWICH', 39.97, -81.79, 39.97, -81.79, 'Upper level low pressure was in place across the upper Ohio Valley on the afternoon of the 12th.  A shortwave rotating around the upper low worked with existing instability to produce scattered thunderstorms in Ohio, with one storm in Muskingum County becoming strong enough to produce large hail.', 'Citizens of Muskingum County reported hail.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (779966, 201808, 13, 1230, 201808, 13, 1230, 130273, 'PENNSYLVANIA', 42, 2018, 'August', 'Hail', 'PBZ', '13-AUG-18 12:30:00', 'EST-5', '13-AUG-18 12:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Trained Spotter', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 'W', 'INDIANOLA', 1, 'W', 'INDIANOLA', 40.57, -79.86, 40.57, -79.86, 'A lingering upper level low pressure system drifting across Pennsylvania promoted low convective temperatures, allowing numerous showers and thunderstorms to develop, which concentrated near disturbances around the upper low.  Decent instability and strong low-level lapse rates promoted damaging wind gusts and hail.  Moisture overall was not excessive for August, but thanks to weak flow near the upper low, storm motion was generally 10 knots or less.  This promoted a few instances of flash flooding as well.', 'Citizens of Allegheny County reported hail.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (778479, 201810, 5, 230, 201810, 5, 235, 129950, 'KENTUCKY', 21, 2018, 'October', 'Debris Flow', 'JKL', '05-OCT-18 02:30:00', 'EST-5', '05-OCT-18 02:35:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SSE', 'HARDSHELL', 1, 'SSE', 'HARDSHELL', 37.4591, -83.2425, 37.4591, -83.242, 'A slow southward moving frontal boundary brought abundant rainfall to southeastern Breathitt and western Knott Counties early this morning. Repeated rounds of heavy rainfall trained along this boundary as it became nearly stationary, resulting in 3 to 4 inches of rain. A couple of roads from Hardshell to Laurel Fork had flowing water across them, while a mudslide was also reported near Hardshell.', 'Dispatch reported a mudslide near Hardshell as repeated rounds of heavy rainfall occurred.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (780834, 201810, 8, 1420, 201810, 8, 1420, 130435, 'ATLANTIC SOUTH', 87, 2018, 'October', 'Marine Thunderstorm Wind', 'MLB', '08-OCT-18 14:20:00', 'EST-5', '08-OCT-18 14:20:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Other Federal Agency', 34, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 14, 'NNE', 'PLAYALINDA BEACH', 14, 'NNE', 'PLAYALINDA BEACH', 28.74, -80.7, 28.74, -80.7, 'The large circulation around Hurricane Michael located near the western tip of Cuba combined with high pressure to the north to initiate quick-moving showers with gusty winds over the Atlantic. The squalls spread onshore the coast from Volusia to St. Lucie counties between early afternoon and late evening, producing frequent wind gusts of 35 to 40 knots.', 'USAF wind tower 0019 measured a peak gust of 34 knots from the southeast as a squall moved onshore from the Atlantic.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (780836, 201810, 8, 1705, 201810, 8, 1705, 130435, 'ATLANTIC SOUTH', 87, 2018, 'October', 'Marine Thunderstorm Wind', 'MLB', '08-OCT-18 17:05:00', 'EST-5', '08-OCT-18 17:05:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Other Federal Agency', 38, 'MG', NULL, NULL, 9, 'ENE', 'PLAYALINDA BEACH', 9, 'ENE', 'PLAYALINDA BEACH', 28.6, -80.64, 28.6, -80.64, 'The large circulation around Hurricane Michael located near the western tip of Cuba combined with high pressure to the north to initiate quick-moving showers with gusty winds over the Atlantic. The squalls spread onshore the coast from Volusia to St. Lucie counties between early afternoon and late evening, producing frequent wind gusts of 35 to 40 knots.', 'USAF wind tower 0311 measured a peak gust of 38 knots from the east as a squall moved onshore from the Atlantic.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (782498, 201810, 2, 1730, 201810, 2, 1730, 130766, 'WEST VIRGINIA', 54, 2018, 'October', 'Hail', 'RLX', '02-OCT-18 17:30:00', 'EST-5', '02-OCT-18 17:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', 0.88, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 'NW', 'MC WHORTER', 2, 'NW', 'MC WHORTER', 39.14, -80.41, 39.14, -80.41, 'The combination of an upper level shortwave trough and a stalled surface boundary produced strong to severe thunderstorms and isolated flash flooding across north central West Virginia on the 2nd.', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790476, 201810, 12, 130, 201810, 12, 230, 131827, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'LWX', '12-OCT-18 01:30:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 02:30:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'GREAT MILLS', 1, 'N', 'GREAT MILLS', 38.2434, -76.5049, 38.2436, -76.5029, 'After Hurricane Michael made landfall near Mexico Beach, Florida with maximum sustained winds of 155 mph and minimum central pressure of 919 mb on Wednesday October 10th, it weakened to a tropical storm as it tracked northeastward through Georgia and South Carolina. The tropical storm then tracked across North Carolina and southeast Virginia Thursday and Thursday night, October 11th, emerging back into the Atlantic Ocean early Friday morning, October 12th. As the system passed south of the region, heavy rain then caused instances of flooding, and strong winds brought down trees. Across Maryland, maximum sustained winds reached 38 mph, peak wind gusts reached 62 mph, and some rainfall totals were observed in excess of 6 inches.', 'The stream gauge on Saint Marys River near Great  Mills touched minor flood stage of 6 feet at 1:30 AM EST. At 6 feet,Flat Iron Road begins to flood with water in some yards.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (786899, 201810, 2, 1643, 201810, 2, 2100, 131544, 'CONNECTICUT', 9, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'BOX', '02-OCT-18 16:43:00', 'EST-5', '02-OCT-18 21:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Amateur Radio', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SSW', 'COTTAGE GROVE', 1, 'S', 'COTTAGE GROVE', 41.8046, -72.7047, 41.8028, -72.7044, 'Cool northeast flow was in place across the region but a warm front attempted to move north through Connecticut just as a mid-level short wave trough was approaching. Low pressure from the Midwest moved along the boundary, crossing New England at night. Instability and unseasonably high moisture generated damaging thunderstorms and downpours. A brief tornado also touched down in Mansfield, CT during the early evening.', 'At 4:43 PM EST, a car was reported trapped in flood waters on Lyme Street near the Breakthrough II Magnet School.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (788256, 201810, 2, 1631, 201810, 2, 1631, 131754, 'PENNSYLVANIA', 42, 2018, 'October', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'PBZ', '02-OCT-18 16:31:00', 'EST-5', '02-OCT-18 16:31:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 1, 'ENE', 'YOUNDWOOD', 1, 'ENE', 'YOUNDWOOD', 40.2541, -79.5595, 40.2541, -79.5595, 'A low amplitude, positively-tilted shortwave trough crossed the eastern Great Lakes/upper Ohio Valley region on October 2nd.  Showers and thunderstorms developed in a moist, unstable air mass across eastern Ohio, northwestern Pennsylvania, and northern West Virginia. Despite expansive early morning cloud cover, some clearing was observed, allowing for a substantial buildup of instability, while dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s illustrated the available moisture.  The crossing shortwave and associated mid-level jet resulted in 40 knots of deep layer shear and a wind profile which favored discrete cells. As a result, several supercells and tornadic signatures were observed.||A total of six tornadoes were observed in the Pittsburgh forecast area, with ratings ranging from EF-0 to EF-2.  The most significant damage was observed with a tornado that resulted in damage to homes and gravestones in the vicinity of Jefferson Cemetery in Jefferson County.  No deaths or injuries were reported from these tornadoes. Several reports of hail up to golf ball size were also received, as well as reports of straight-line wind damage.||In all, 14 tornadoes occurred in the state of Pennsylvania on this day.  The previous record number (since 1950) of tornadoes on an October day was four on October 5, 1979.  Overall, there are only three days with more Pennsylvania tornadoes recorded:  May 31st to early June 1st, 1998 (23 tornadoes), June 2nd, 1998 (22), and May 31st, 1985 (21).', 'County 911 officials reported that multiple trees were blown down, blocking Waycross Road.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (788632, 201810, 11, 1415, 201810, 12, 305, 130716, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'RNK', '11-OCT-18 14:15:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 03:05:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'River/Stream Gage', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NNE', 'LAFAYETTE', 1, 'NNE', 'LAFAYETTE', 37.2363, -80.2139, 37.2384, -80.2136, 'Hurricane Michael made landfall along the Florida panhandle as major hurricane (Category 4 on the Saffir-Simpson scale) on Wednesday afternoon, October 10th, 2018, then tracked northeastward with the northern portion of the storm circulation tracking across portions of Southside, Virginia Thursday afternoon, the 11th. A deep flow of tropical moisture into the Appalachians and central mid Atlantic region ahead of the storm began to generate excessive rainfall across portions of the region as early as Wednesday evening, mainly along the Interstate 77 corridor, causing some degree of flash flooding and otherwise saturating the ground even before the arrival of the rainfall directly associated with Michael.||As the storm circulation approached on Thursday, a cold front moving in from the west and interacted with the storm and enhanced rainfall especially east of Interstate 81. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4 to 8 inches were reported, along with local amounts over 10 inches, mainly from the mountains of North Carolina up through Southside Virginia. This resulted in significant flash flooding with flash flood emergencies issued for the city of Roanoke, as well as Roanoke County, and southern Pittsylvania county including the city of Danville.  Unfortunately, there were five fatalities as vehicles were swept away by flash flood waters in Pittsylvania County, Danville, and Charlotte County, Virginia.||The massive volume of water also then generated rapid rises on rivers including the New, Roanoke, and Dan. Major flooding was reported at several river gage locations along with a couple of record crests. There were a few landslides reported as well, blocking roads and in one case destroying a shed.||In addition, as the northern portion of the storm circulation moved across the Piedmont, bands of heavier rains/convection developed and mixed strong winds down to the surface.  Many trees were blown down in the water logged soil especially across Pittsylvania, Halifax and Charlotte counties of Virginia where rainfall was heaviest.  Despite some initial reports of tornado sightings, damage surveys revealed no conclusive evidence of tornadic circulations and judged damage was caused by straight line winds.', 'The Roanoke River at Lafayatte (LAAV2) crested at 12.44 feet (16100 cfs) on the evening of the 11th. Moderate flood stage is 11 feet. It was the 6th highest reading at this location since USGS records began in 1944 and the highest since September 28, 2004 (12.45 feet) with the remains of Hurricane Jeanne. In terms of flood frequency it fell between a 10- and 25-year event (0.10-0.04 annual chance flood).');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (788257, 201810, 2, 1634, 201810, 2, 1634, 131754, 'PENNSYLVANIA', 42, 2018, 'October', 'Thunderstorm Wind', 'PBZ', '02-OCT-18 16:34:00', 'EST-5', '02-OCT-18 16:34:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '1.00K', '0.00K', '911 Call Center', 50, 'EG', NULL, NULL, 2, 'SSE', 'RED MILL', 2, 'SSE', 'RED MILL', 41.1411, -78.8066, 41.1411, -78.8066, 'A low amplitude, positively-tilted shortwave trough crossed the eastern Great Lakes/upper Ohio Valley region on October 2nd.  Showers and thunderstorms developed in a moist, unstable air mass across eastern Ohio, northwestern Pennsylvania, and northern West Virginia. Despite expansive early morning cloud cover, some clearing was observed, allowing for a substantial buildup of instability, while dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s illustrated the available moisture.  The crossing shortwave and associated mid-level jet resulted in 40 knots of deep layer shear and a wind profile which favored discrete cells. As a result, several supercells and tornadic signatures were observed.||A total of six tornadoes were observed in the Pittsburgh forecast area, with ratings ranging from EF-0 to EF-2.  The most significant damage was observed with a tornado that resulted in damage to homes and gravestones in the vicinity of Jefferson Cemetery in Jefferson County.  No deaths or injuries were reported from these tornadoes. Several reports of hail up to golf ball size were also received, as well as reports of straight-line wind damage.||In all, 14 tornadoes occurred in the state of Pennsylvania on this day.  The previous record number (since 1950) of tornadoes on an October day was four on October 5, 1979.  Overall, there are only three days with more Pennsylvania tornadoes recorded:  May 31st to early June 1st, 1998 (23 tornadoes), June 2nd, 1998 (22), and May 31st, 1985 (21).', 'County 911 officials reported uprooted trees at the intersection of 4th Street and Osborne Avenue.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790473, 201810, 12, 310, 201810, 12, 510, 131827, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'LWX', '12-OCT-18 03:10:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 05:10:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'SE', 'CROFTON', 1, 'ESE', 'CROFTON', 38.9902, -76.6411, 38.9929, -76.6297, 'After Hurricane Michael made landfall near Mexico Beach, Florida with maximum sustained winds of 155 mph and minimum central pressure of 919 mb on Wednesday October 10th, it weakened to a tropical storm as it tracked northeastward through Georgia and South Carolina. The tropical storm then tracked across North Carolina and southeast Virginia Thursday and Thursday night, October 11th, emerging back into the Atlantic Ocean early Friday morning, October 12th. As the system passed south of the region, heavy rain then caused instances of flooding, and strong winds brought down trees. Across Maryland, maximum sustained winds reached 38 mph, peak wind gusts reached 62 mph, and some rainfall totals were observed in excess of 6 inches.', 'MD-450 Defense Highway was closed due to flooding near Huntwood Drive.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790474, 201810, 12, 641, 201810, 12, 841, 131827, 'MARYLAND', 24, 2018, 'October', 'Flood', 'LWX', '12-OCT-18 06:41:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 08:41:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Department of Highways', NULL, NULL, 'Heavy Rain', NULL, 1, 'NNW', 'INDIAN HD MRYLND ARP', 1, 'NNW', 'INDIAN HD MRYLND ARP', 38.6081, -77.0733, 38.6089, -77.0729, 'After Hurricane Michael made landfall near Mexico Beach, Florida with maximum sustained winds of 155 mph and minimum central pressure of 919 mb on Wednesday October 10th, it weakened to a tropical storm as it tracked northeastward through Georgia and South Carolina. The tropical storm then tracked across North Carolina and southeast Virginia Thursday and Thursday night, October 11th, emerging back into the Atlantic Ocean early Friday morning, October 12th. As the system passed south of the region, heavy rain then caused instances of flooding, and strong winds brought down trees. Across Maryland, maximum sustained winds reached 38 mph, peak wind gusts reached 62 mph, and some rainfall totals were observed in excess of 6 inches.', 'Pomfret Road at Livingston Road was closed due to high water.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790614, 201810, 11, 1200, 201810, 12, 0, 132113, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Heavy Rain', 'AKQ', '11-OCT-18 12:00:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 00:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'N', 'KING & QUEEN CHURCH', 0, 'N', 'KING & QUEEN CHURCH', 37.67, -76.88, 37.67, -76.88, 'Tropical Cyclone Michael tracked from South Carolina northeast and off the Mid Atlantic Coast from Thursday morning, October 11 into early Friday morning, October 12. The Tropical Cyclone produced heavy rain across much of central and south central Virginia, the Virginia Northern Neck, and the Middle Peninsula. Rainfall totals ranged between two and nine inches.', 'Rainfall totals ranged between 3.00 inches and 6.00 inches across the county.');
INSERT INTO public.stormevents_east VALUES (790615, 201810, 11, 1200, 201810, 12, 0, 132113, 'VIRGINIA', 51, 2018, 'October', 'Heavy Rain', 'AKQ', '11-OCT-18 12:00:00', 'EST-5', '12-OCT-18 00:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 0, '0.00K', '0.00K', 'Public', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'N', 'WEST PT', 0, 'N', 'WEST PT', 37.53, -76.8, 37.53, -76.8, 'Tropical Cyclone Michael tracked from South Carolina northeast and off the Mid Atlantic Coast from Thursday morning, October 11 into early Friday morning, October 12. The Tropical Cyclone produced heavy rain across much of central and south central Virginia, the Virginia Northern Neck, and the Middle Peninsula. Rainfall totals ranged between two and nine inches.', 'Rainfall total of 4.94 inches was reported at Romancoke (2 W). Rainfall total of 4.82 inches was reported at West Point.');
